lique.fr\nL215: \nL216:   * 【37†Rôle et pouvoirs de l'Assemblée nationale†www.assemblee-nationale.fr】\nL217: \nL218: Assemblée nationale\nL219: \nL220:   * 【38†Qu'est ce qu'un député ?†www.vie-publique.fr】\nL221: \nL222: Vie-publique.fr\nL223:   * 【18†Annexe à la loi n°86-825 du 11 juillet 1986 sur les circonscriptions électorales des législatives†www.legifrance.gouv.fr】\nL224: \nL225: Legifrance\nL226: \nL227:   * 【16†Vote électronique†www.diplomatie.gouv.fr】\nL228: \nL229: Ministère chargé de l'Europe et des affaires étrangères\nL230: \nL231:   * 【20†Résultats des élections†www.archives-resultats-elections.interieur.gouv.fr】\nL232: \nL233: Ministère chargé de l'intérieur\nL234: ## Cette page vous a-t-elle été utile ?\nL235: \nL236:   * 1\nL237: \nL238:   * 2\nL239: \nL240:   * 3\nL241: \nL242:   * 4\nL243: \nL244:   * 5\nL245: \nL246: Pas du tout\nL247: \nL248: Un peu\nL249: \nL250: Moyen\nL251: \nL252: Beaucoup\nL253: \nL254: Parfait !\nL255: \nL256: L’équipe Service Public vous remercie\nL257: \nL258: 【39†Je vais sur la page d’accueil】\nL259: \nL260: [Button: Masquer le message ]\nL261: \nL262: Vous avez noté 1 sur 5 : Pas du tout\nL263: \nL264: Vous avez noté 2 sur 5 : Un peu\nL265: \nL266: Vous avez noté 3 sur 5 : Moyen\nL267: \nL268: Vous avez noté 4 sur 5 : Beaucoup\nL269: \nL270: Vous avez noté 5 sur 5 : Parfait !\nL271: \nL272: L’équipe Service Public vous remercie pour vos remarques utiles à l'amélioration du site.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrinciples of Cabinet decision-making | Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (DPMC) (https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/5-cabinet-decision-making/principles-cabinet-decision-making)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/5-cabinet-decision-making/principles-cabinet-decision-making\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 117\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: Back to top anchor\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (DPMC)】\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Page url】\nL7: \nL8: # Principles of Cabinet decision-making\nL9: #### Items for consideration by Cabinet\nL10: 5.11 As a general rule, Ministers should put before their colleagues the sorts of issues on which they themselves would wish to be consulted. Ministers should keep their colleagues informed about matters of public interest, importance, or controversy. Where there is uncertainty about the type of consideration needed, Ministers should seek advice from the Prime Minister or the Secretary of the Cabinet.\nL11: Similarly, agencies should seek advice from the office of the portfolio Minister, or from the Cabinet Office.\nL12: 5.12 The following matters must be submitted to Cabinet (through the appropriate committee):\nL13:   1. significant policy issues;\nL14:   2. controversial matters;\nL15:   3. proposals that affect the government's financial position, or important financial commitments, including proposals seeking additional financial resources;\nL16:   4. proposals that affect New Zealand's constitutional arrangements (see paragraph 【3†5.77】);\nL17:   5. matters concerning the machinery of government;\nL18:   6. discussion and consultation documents (before their public release), other than those relating to minor or technical matters that fall within existing policy and that have been authorised for release by the Minister;\nL19:   7. reports of a substantive nature relating to government policy or government agencies;\nL20:   8. proposals involving new legislation (see 【4†chapter 7】 and the 【5†CabGuide】);\nL21:   9. government responses to select committee recommendations and Law Commission reports (see paragraphs 【6†7.22】 and 【7†7.123 - 7.126】, and the CabGuide);\nL22:   10. matters concerning the portfolio interests of a number of Ministers (particularly where agreement cannot be reached);\nL23:   11. significant statutory decisions (see paragraphs 【8†5.34 - 5.38】);\nL24:   12. all appointments made by Ministers or by the Governor-General on the advice of a Minister (see the CabGuide);\nL25:   13. international treaties (see paragraphs 【9†5.78 - 5.82】) and significant matters concerning New Zealand's international relationships, security, foreign policy and deployments offshore; and\nL26:   14. any proposals to amend the provisions of the Cabinet Manual.\nL27: 5.13 Matters that should not, as a general rule, be brought to Cabinet include:\nL28: \nL29:   1. matters concerning the day-to-day management of a portfolio that have been delegated to an agency;\nL30:   2. operational (non-policy) statutory functions; or\nL31:   3. the exercise of statutory decision-making powers (within existing policy) concerning individuals.\nL32: \nL33: It may, nonetheless, be appropriate to bring an item falling into this list to Cabinet's attention if it is significant or likely to be controversial.\nL34: #### Consultation\nL35: ##### Portfolio consultation\nL36: \nL37: 5.14 Ministers are expected to consult relevant ministerial colleagues before submitting papers that deal with significant or potentially controversial matters, or that affect other Ministers’ portfolio interests. Where appropriate consultation has not taken place, a paper may be deferred from a particular meeting. In particular, Ministers are required to consult:\nL38:   1. the Minister of Finance on all proposals seeking additional resources;\nL39:   2. the Minister for the Public Service on machinery of government issues;\nL40:   3. the Minister of Foreign Affairs on all proposals relating to international treaties and New Zealand's strategic international interests;\nL41:   4. the Minister of Justice on all proposals affecting constitutional arrangements, including the Treaty of Waitangi, and human rights;\nL42:   5. the Attorney-General on all proposals raising significant legal issues; and\nL43:   6. the Attorney-General or the Minister(s) with responsibilities relating to Treaty of Waitangi settlement obligations and/or Māori-Crown relations on any proposal with potential impacts on existing Treaty of Waitangi settlements.\nL44: 5.15 The 【5†CabGuide】 provides detailed guidance on consultation requirements.\nL45: ##### Political consultation\nL46: \nL47: 5.16 In a coalition or minority government, the coalition or support partners are likely to agree to specific consultation procedures, which may be approved by Cabinet and promulgated by Cabinet Office circulars. Ministers are responsible for ensuring that consultation is undertaken in accordance with any agreements entered into between political parties.\nL48: 5.17 Careful planning, good faith, and a “no surprises” approach are key to making the arrangements work effectively. All Ministers and chief executives need to be familiar with the current arrangements and ensure that they have processes in place to implement them. Managing the consultation processes and other aspects of the relationships may take some time. Ministers and officials should factor the time required for consultation into their planning on each issue.\nL49: 5.18 For more details on any current agreements between political parties, see the 【5†CabGuide】 and relevant 【10†Cabinet Office circulars】, available on the Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet's 【11†website】.\nL50: ##### Agency consultation\nL51: 5.19 Almost all policy proposals have implications for other government agencies. The initiating agency or another agency with policy responsibility and the portfolio Minister must ensure that all other agencies affected by a proposal are consulted at the earliest possible stage, and that their comments are taken into account and reflected in the paper as appropriate.\nL52: Consultation may sometimes be needed with bodies that have an advisory role, such as the 【12†Office of the Privacy Commissioner†www.privacy.org.nz】. See the 【5†CabGuide】 for full guidance on consultation procedures, and paragraph 【13†5.33】 for guidance on collective decision-making and government agencies.\nL53: 5.20 Agencies should work closely with one another when developing advice in preparation for papers for Cabinet and Cabinet committees and, where possible, reach consensus on advice and proposals submitted to the portfolio Minister. If consensus is not reached, a Cabinet paper may reflect different ministerial positions or agency advice so that Cabinet can take decisions based on all the facts available and an appreciation of the options.\nL54: ##### Statutory consultation processes\nL55: \nL56: 5.21 In some cases, legislation may prescribe a consultation process to be followed before the Minister can make a statutory decision. The Minister and agency concerned should ensure that the required consultation has taken place (see also paragraphs 【8†5.34 - 5.38】).\nL57: ##### Public consultation\nL58: 5.22 A critical consideration in developing workable and effective policy is assessing the need for, and the timing of, engagement with Māori (including relevant iwi, hapū, and whānau), the public, and relevant stakeholder groups. Agencies should bear in mind that consultation may be undertaken face to face, using discussion documents, or using digital consultation tools. See the 【5†CabGuide】 for information on public and stakeholder consultation, including engagement with Māori.\nL59: #### Confidentiality\nL60: 5.23 Discussion at Cabinet and Cabinet committee meetings is informal and confidential. Ministers and officials should not disclose proposals likely to be considered at forthcoming meetings, outside Cabinet-approved consultation procedures. Nor should they disclose or record the nature or content of the discussions or the views of individual Ministers or officials expressed at the meeting itself.\nL61: The detail of discussion at Cabinet and Cabinet committee meetings is not formally recorded, or included in the minutes.\nL62: #### Collective responsibility\nL63: ##### Principle of collective responsibility\nL64: 5.24 The principle of collective responsibility underpins the system of Cabinet government. It reflects democratic principle: the House expresses its confidence in the collective whole of government, rather than in individual Ministers. Similarly, the Governor-General, in acting on ministerial advice, needs to be confident that individual Ministers represent official government policy. In all areas of their work, therefore, Ministers represent and implement government policy.\nL65: 5.25 Acceptance of ministerial office (whether inside or outside Cabinet) means accepting collective responsibility. Issues are often debated vigorously in the confidential setting of Cabinet meetings, although consensus is usually reached and votes are rarely taken. Once Cabinet makes a decision, Ministers must support it (except as provided in paragraphs 【14†5.27 - 5.29】), regardless of their personal views and whether or not they were at the meeting concerned.\nL66: ##### Collective responsibility and coalition Ministers\nL67: \nL68: 5.26 In a coalition government, Ministers are expected to show careful judgement when referring to party policy that differs from government policy. Subject to paragraphs 【14†5.27 - 5.30】 a Minister's support and responsibility for the collective government position must always be clear (see paragraph 【15†6.18】 on the difference between coalition and other formal arrangements between political parties).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMinisterial Code (HTML) - GOV.UK (https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ministerial-code/ministerial-code)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ministerial-code/ministerial-code\",\"lineno\":n